1. Background Reflection
Recently, I used AI to generate a low-level malicious script containing persistence mechanisms. To prevent any potential misuse, I have completely destroyed the malicious source code. This document records the defensive and remediation logic I have summarized to counter such threats.

2. Watchdog Termination Logic

1. Mechanism Analysis: Basic malicious scripts often use a continuous loop (e.g., while True) to check the system every few seconds. If the script is removed, it instantly recreates itself.
2. Defense Logic: You cannot simply delete the registry keys first. You must cut the root.
   · Step A: Create a specific "kill-switch sentinel file" (e.g., .stop) to break the loop condition.
   · Step B: Traverse and forcefully terminate the script process and all its derived child processes (Process Tree Termination).
   · Step C: Only after the processes are dead should you proceed with system cleanup.

3. Registry Persistence Hijack Remediation

1. Standard Startup: Scan and remove abnormal key values under HKCU/HKLM\...\Run and RunOnce.
2. Deep-Level Persistence: Inspect BootExecute (Session Manager) and Winlogon (User Login Hijack). Crucial Note: When cleaning Winlogon, the trailing comma structure of the original value must be strictly preserved to prevent OS corruption.
3. SafeBoot Injection: Remove fake services injected into SafeBoot to prevent the malware from resurrecting in Safe Mode.

4. Driver Tampering Reversal

1. Mouse Filter Driver (UpperFilters): Malware may inject a fake filter driver name into the registry of mouse class devices. Remediation: Read the value, remove the malicious string, and if the list becomes empty, delete the key entirely to restore system defaults.
2. Mouse Queue Parameters: Restore the reduced MouseDataQueueSize and SampleRate under mouclass\Parameters back to system defaults (usually 100) to prevent hardware malfunction.

5. Conclusion
Through this experience, I deeply realized that while AI can easily generate destructive logical frameworks, true security defense requires solid fundamental knowledge of operating systems (such as C language and Kernel Callbacks). I will take this as a lesson and focus on legitimate cybersecurity defense technologies in the future.
